A. No CATV franchise shall be issued except on a written application and upon a form approved by the city council.
B. The form shall set forth such facts in detail as the city council may deem appropriate including:
1. If the applicant is an individual, partnership, or unincorporated association, its statement shall contain the names and addresses of all persons (including corporations) having a proprietary or equitable interest in and to the prospective franchise's business operation, and in and to the prospective franchise if awarded to the proposer. The term equitable interest shall include all assignment for value, as well as all contingent assignments of any right or privilege under the prospective franchise, and shall also include any benefit, payment, or emolument whatsoever resulting from the grant of a franchise under this chapter;
2. If the applicant is a nonpublic corporation, the statement shall furnish, additionally, the names and addresses of the officers, directors, and shareholders of the said corporation together with the number of shares held by each shareholder and the total number of issued shares;
3. If the applicant is a publicly held corporation, as defined by the rules and regulations of the securities and exchange commission, the statement shall contain the states in which incorporated and/or qualified to do business and the names and addresses of the officers and directors of the corporation;
4. A full disclosure of the ownership of the facilities to be used in rendering the service;
5. The source of funds for operation of the system respecting the installation and maintenance of all CATV facilities; and shall demonstrate the financial ability to provide and extend service to proposed subscribers at a reasonable cost;
6. A detailed schedule of the rates to be charged for the services offered, the facilities to be employed and the general routes of the cables used in redistributing signals, the service area or areas, the commencement and completion dates of construction of the CATV system and the proposed dates the service will be available to the area or areas named.
C. The council may request such other information as it may deem appropriate.
D. All applications shall be open to public inspection, shall be kept on file a reasonable length of time at the discretion of the council, and any intentional misrepresentation in an application shall be grounds for its rejection or for termination of the franchise.
E. All applications shall be considered firm offers to the city, shall be signed and verified by the applicants whose relationship to the applicant shall be set forth and shall bind the applicant to the provisions thereof.
F. An application for the privilege of a franchise shall be submitted to the city with an initial nonrefundable application fee of $2,500.00. This article shall not apply to renewals of existing franchises. In the event of a proposed sale or transfer to a new entity, except that allowed under Section 8-24-270(B), the new entity shall pay to the city a nonrefundable application fee of $2,500.00 and shall go through the process for an application for a CATV franchise.
G. Performance bond requirements include:
1. Each grantee shall file with its application for a franchise, and at all times thereafter until the filing of a final notice of completion pursuant to Section 8-24-140 and approval by the city public works director, maintain in full force and effect, an acceptable corporate surety bond issued by a surety licensed therefor by the State of Georgia, in an amount equal to the greater of (i) the product of the number of miles of aerial plant (to the nearest one-tenth of a mile) in the franchise area and $18,000.00, or (ii) the product of the number of miles of underground plant (to the nearest one-tenth of a mile) in the franchise area and $32,000.00, effective for the entire term of the franchise and conditioned that in the event the grantee shall fail to comply with any one or more of the provisions of the franchise whether or not the franchise is terminated, then there shall be recoverable jointly and severally from the principal and surety of such bond, any damages, delinquent franchise fees, compensation and costs of repairing or completing the cable television system, and compensation, and costs of removal or abandonment of property and repair of streets and other public or private improvements, up to the full amount of the bond; said condition to be a continuing obligation for the duration of the franchise and thereafter until the grantee has satisfied all of its obligations which may have arisen from the acceptance of the franchise or from its exercise of any privileges thereunder.
2. Neither the provisions of this section, any bond accepted pursuant thereto, nor any damages recovered hereunder shall be construed to excuse faithful performance by the grantee or to limit the liability of the grantee under the franchise, or for damages, either to the full amount of the cancellation by the surety during the term of the franchise, whether for failure to pay a premium or otherwise, without 30 calendar days' advance certified written notice mailed by the surety to the city clerk. The form of the bond and surety shall be subject to the approval by the city council.
(Code 1978, § 8-6005; Ord. No. 5390, § 3, 12/20/94; Ord. No. 5575, 6/12/96)

1515:
A. A grantee shall construct, operate and maintain the CATV system subject to the supervision of all of the authorities of the city who have jurisdiction in such matters and in strict compliance with all laws, ordinances and departmental rules and regulations.
B. The CATV system and all parts thereof shall be subject to the right of periodic inspection by the city.
C. The city council may from time to time issue such reasonable rules and regulations for the health, safety and welfare of the citizens of the city concerning the construction, operation and maintenance of the CATV system as are consistent with the provisions of this chapter.
D. If at any time the powers of the city council or any agency or official of the city are transferred by law to any other board, authority, agency or official the transferee shall have the powers, rights and duties previously vested under this chapter or by law in the council or any agency or official of the city.
E. No direct charge shall be made to subscribers for normal and routine maintenance. Each grantee shall maintain a service department or personnel who shall:
1. Install underground cable within ten working days of receipt of request to install said facilities;
2. Respond to subscribers' request for service, advice, and routine inquiries within 24 hours after receipt of the request, including holidays and weekends;
3. Notwithstanding Subsection (2), respond and repair any service outage that is within grantee control within 24 hours, including holidays and weekends;
4. Respond to any other complaints or inquiries, other than those in Subsections (1), (2) or (3) within 48 hours after the receipt of the request;
5. Notwithstanding Subsections (1) through (4), inclusive, if any failure of grantee to carry out its obligations hereunder or to meet time schedules herein shall be due to force majeure, such failure shall be excused and any time deadlines established shall be extended for a period of time equal to the period of delay which resulted from force majeure;
6. Grantee shall at all times use diligent efforts to carry out its obligations hereunder in a timely manner.
F. Each grantee shall maintain a written record or log as follows:
1. Listing the date of any informal citizen complaint relating to the cable system, the name of the complainant, the nature of the informal citizen complaint, and the date and nature of the grantee's response;
2. The log is to be maintained at grantee's local business office for a period of two years from the date of the complaint and shall be available for inspection by the public and the city during normal business hours; and
3. Grantee shall submit, upon request of the city manager or individual designated by council, said log or record to the city manager for council's review every six months;
4. Informal citizen complaints may be made in writing, by telephone or any other form of contract.
G. For purposes of this chapter, the term "informal citizen complaint" shall not include routine requests for service, repairs or advice on the operation of a subscriber's converter or television set; provided, however, an informal citizen complaint may relate back to routine request for service, repairs or advice which was not satisfactorily resolved or which arises out of an incident related to the request.
H. Any citizen of the city who is not satisfied with the response of grantee(s) to any complaint may complain through written letter to the city public works director who may informally inquire into the complaint in an effort to resolve the matter. The citizen in his letter shall provide to the city public works director the following information:
1. The name and address of the complainant;
2. The name of the cable system against which the complaint is made; and
3. A complete statement of facts upon which the complaint is made.
The citizen's letter to the city public works director must be postmarked or hand delivered within 90 days of the original informal complaint to grantee; otherwise, the informal complaint shall be deemed abandoned.
If a citizen has received an unsatisfactory result after 30 days from receipt of the citizen's letter by the city public works director, the citizen may file a "formal citizen complaint" in accordance with Section 8-24-320 entitled "Inquiry and complaint procedures."
(Code 1978, § 8-6019; Ord. No. 5390, § 3, 12/20/94; Ord. No. 5575, 6/12/96)

